---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: 'Rosary Materials Guide for Wholesale Buyers',
  description:
    'Compare wood, glass, acrylic, stone, zinc alloy, stainless steel, brass, plating, cord, and other rosary materials before sourcing.',
};

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Rosary Materials Guide for Wholesale Buyers',
  description: metadata.description,
  dateModified: '2026-08-08',
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://ouooo.com/guides/choosing-rosary-materials',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'OUOOO', url: 'https://ouooo.com' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'OUOOO', url: 'https://ouooo.com' },
};

const comparison = [
  [
    'Wood',
    'Beads, crosses, ornaments',
    'Warm, light, traditional',
    'Species, solid or imitation, coating, grain variation',
  ],
  [
    'Glass',
    'Beads and decorative details',
    'Reflective, formal, substantial',
    'Molded or cut, coating, color consistency, breakage risk',
  ],
  [
    'Acrylic / resin',
    'Beads, charms, colorful components',
    'Light, economical, broad color range',
    'Exact polymer, molding quality, color and surface finish',
  ],
  [
    'Natural stone',
    'Premium beads and bracelets',
    'Distinctive pattern and weight',
    'Natural, dyed, reconstructed, or imitation material',
  ],
  [
    'Zinc alloy',
    'Crucifixes, medals, centerpieces, charms',
    'Detailed casting and flexible finishes',
    'Alloy, plating, restricted substances, finish durability',
  ],
  [
    'Stainless steel',
    'Chain, findings, crosses, medals',
    'Corrosion resistant and durable',
    'Grade such as 201, 304, or 316; solid steel or plated look',
  ],
  [
    'Brass',
    'Detailed components and premium findings',
    'Good forming and plating base',
    'Solid brass or brass-plated alloy, lacquer, lead requirements',
  ],
  [
    'Iron / carbon steel',
    'Chain, eye pins, jump rings',
    'Strong and cost effective',
    'Coating coverage, rust protection, magnetic response',
  ],
];

const beadMaterials = [
  [
    'Wood',
    'Wood is lightweight, warm to the touch, and well suited to traditional, missionary, men’s, and natural-style collections. Grain, knots, density, and shade can vary between beads and production batches. Buyers should confirm whether a product uses solid wood, laminated wood, compressed wood, or another material with a wood-grain finish. Also specify staining, paint, wax, lacquer, engraving, and acceptable color variation.',
  ],
  [
    'Glass and crystal-style glass',
    'Glass beads generally feel heavier and more reflective than wood or plastic. They can be transparent, opaque, faceted, pearl-coated, painted, or internally colored. “Crystal” is sometimes used as a style or trade description, so buyers who require a particular glass composition or cut should request written confirmation. Check hole quality, edge chipping, coating adhesion, color consistency, and breakage protection in packaging.',
  ],
  [
    'Acrylic, plastic, resin, PVC, and silicone',
    'These names describe different material families and should not be treated as interchangeable. Acrylic and other molded plastics are useful for lightweight, colorful, price-sensitive programs. Resin can support embedded images, handcrafted effects, and complex shapes. Soft PVC and silicone are used for flexible charms and children-oriented designs. Confirm the exact polymer, hardness, odor, colorfastness, molding marks, and destination-market requirements.',
  ],
  [
    'Natural stone and stone-look beads',
    'Natural stone offers distinctive color, pattern, cool touch, and premium weight, but natural variation is expected. Some beads may be dyed, heat-treated, stabilized, reconstructed, composite, or made from glass or resin to imitate stone. If authenticity is central to your product claim, define the accepted material and treatment in writing and consider independent testing.',
  ],
  [
    'Ceramic, porcelain, shell, and pearl-style materials',
    'Ceramic and porcelain beads allow painted devotional patterns and a smooth, substantial feel, but require careful packaging against impact. Pearl-style products may use coated glass, coated plastic, shell-based material, or genuine cultured pearl. The appearance can be similar in photos while cost, weight, and care requirements differ greatly.',
  ],
];

const metalMaterials = [
  [
    'Zinc alloy',
    'Zinc alloy is common for cast crucifixes, medals, centerpieces, and decorative charms because it can reproduce complex shapes and accept many surface finishes. The visible antique silver, gold, bronze, or black appearance is usually a finish — not proof of the base metal. Confirm alloy requirements, plating system, topcoat, wear expectations, and limits for lead, cadmium, nickel release, or other restricted substances required by the destination market.',
  ],
  [
    'Stainless steel',
    'Stainless steel is used for chain, pins, jump rings, crosses, medals, and laser-cut components where durability and corrosion resistance are priorities. Stainless steel is a family of grades, not one material. Grade 304 is widely used; grade 316 contains molybdenum and generally offers stronger resistance in chloride-rich environments. Lower-cost grades may behave differently. Ask for the grade and confirm whether every component is stainless steel or only the main visible part.',
  ],
  [
    'Brass',
    'Brass is suitable for stamped, formed, machined, and plated components and can deliver a more premium positioning. It may be polished, antiqued, lacquered, silver plated, or gold toned. “Brass color” does not necessarily mean solid brass. Confirm the base metal, copper alloy requirement, plating, protective coating, and any lead restriction that applies to your market.',
  ],
  [
    'Iron, carbon steel, aluminum, and precious metals',
    'Iron or carbon-steel chain, eye pins, and jump rings can provide good strength at a competitive cost, but exposed areas may corrode if plating or coating is damaged. Aluminum is lightweight and can be anodized, but it has a different feel and hardness from steel. Sterling silver and other precious-metal claims require clear fineness, hallmark, weight, and testing requirements. Do not infer the base metal from color alone.',
  ],
];

const finishChecks = [
  'Base metal and grade.',
  'Plating or coating process and target color.',
  'Gloss, matte, brushed, antiqued, or polished appearance.',
  'Protective topcoat and expected wear environment.',
  'Nickel-free, lead, cadmium, or other compliance requirements where applicable.',
  'Any requested corrosion, tarnish, abrasion, or colorfastness test.',
];

const checklist = [
  'List the exact material required for every major component — not only the beads.',
  'Confirm metal grade, base metal, plating, coating, and surface finish in writing.',
  'State the destination country, intended user, and required regulatory or retailer standard.',
  'Request clear photos, specifications, and a physical sample before a sensitive bulk order.',
  'Approve acceptable color, grain, handmade, and dimensional tolerances.',
  'Use qualified third-party testing when composition or compliance is commercially critical.',
  'Ensure the final quotation or order specification matches the approved sample.',
];
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

  <section class="bg-[#f6f1e8]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-primary">Sourcing guide · Materials</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-4xl font-semibold sm:text-5xl">
        How to choose rosary materials for wholesale sourcing
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-muted">
        A rosary is rarely made from one material. Beads, crucifixes, centerpieces, medals, chain, jump rings, pins,
        cord, coatings, and packaging can all use different materials. The right combination depends on target price,
        weight, appearance, durability, destination-market requirements, and how the product will be used.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
        <a class="btn-primary" href="/contact">Ask about materials</a>
        <a class="btn-secondary" href="/products">Browse products</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
    <div class="rounded-3xl border-l-4 border-primary bg-[#f6f1e8] p-8 sm:p-10">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-primary">Important material verification</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold sm:text-3xl">
        If material composition matters, verify it with us before ordering.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-muted">
        Product photos, color names, weight, and terms such as “gold,” “silver,” “crystal,” or “stainless color” do not
        prove the base material. Ask OUOOO to confirm the exact material, metal grade, plating, finish, and any required
        compliance standard in the quotation or written specification. For critical projects, approve a physical sample
        and request appropriate testing before bulk production.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pb-16">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-primary">At a glance</p>
    <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold">Quick material comparison</h2>
    <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[760px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-[#f6f1e8]">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-4 font-semibold">Material</th>
            <th class="p-4 font-semibold">Typical use</th>
            <th class="p-4 font-semibold">Strength</th>
            <th class="p-4 font-semibold">What buyers should verify</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-black/10">
          {
            comparison.map(([material, use, strength, verify]) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="p-4 font-semibold">{material}</td>
                <td class="p-4 text-muted">{use}</td>
                <td class="p-4 text-muted">{strength}</td>
                <td class="p-4 text-muted">{verify}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-[#f4f5f2] py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-primary">Beads and decorations</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold">Bead and decorative materials</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-8 text-muted">
        The beads are usually the most visible part of a rosary, but the material behind the appearance matters just as
        much for cost, weight, durability, and compliance.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {
          beadMaterials.map(([title, text]) => (
            <article class="rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">{title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-muted">{text}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-primary">Hardware</p>
    <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold">Metals used in crucifixes, medals, chains, and findings</h2>
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
      {
        metalMaterials.map(([title, text]) => (
          <article class="rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-7">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">{title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-muted">{text}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-[#f6f1e8] py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
      <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[.9fr_1.1fr]">
        <div>
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-primary">Finish vs. base material</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold">Plating and finish are not the same as base material</h2>
          <p class="mt-5 leading-8 text-muted">
            Product descriptions often emphasize the visible finish: gold color, silver color, antique silver, oxidized
            black, rose gold, or bronze. A finish may be applied to zinc alloy, brass, steel, or another substrate. For
            repeatable bulk orders, specify:
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="grid gap-3">
          {
            finishChecks.map((item) => (
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-4">
                <span class="mt-1 text-primary">✓</span>
                <span class="leading-7 text-muted">{item}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
    <div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
      <article class="rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-7">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-primary">Assembly</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold">Cord, chain, wire, and assembly materials</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-muted">
          Durability also depends on how the rosary is assembled. Nylon and polyester cord can provide strength and
          consistent color; cotton feels natural but can absorb moisture; waxed cord changes feel and knot behavior;
          elastic is convenient for bracelets but requires stretch-life testing. Metal-linked rosaries depend on wire
          gauge, eye-pin closure, jump-ring construction, chain material, and plating quality. Ask for pull testing or
          repeat-use testing when the application demands it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-7">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-primary">Market and use</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold">Destination-market and product-use requirements</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-muted">
          Requirements can differ for adult jewelry, children’s products, devotional objects, promotional gifts, and
          items sold in different countries. Children’s jewelry can involve specific controls for lead, cadmium, nickel,
          magnets, small parts, coatings, and other hazards. Tell us the destination market and intended user at the
          inquiry stage. The buyer remains responsible for identifying the standards and documentation required for its
          market, while OUOOO can help communicate those requirements to the relevant supply partners.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-[#f4f5f2] py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
      <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-primary">Checklist</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold">Wholesale material verification checklist</h2>
          <ol class="mt-6 space-y-3">
            {
              checklist.map((item, index) => (
                <li class="flex items-start gap-4 rounded-2xl bg-white p-4">
                  <span class="flex h-7 w-7 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[#0f3b2f] text-xs font-semibold text-white">
                    {index + 1}
                  </span>
                  <span class="leading-7 text-muted">{item}</span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col justify-center rounded-3xl border border-black/10 bg-white p-8 sm:p-10">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-primary">Samples</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold sm:text-3xl">Why samples matter</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-muted">
            Photography cannot fully communicate weight, surface temperature, texture, translucency, plating tone,
            sound, flexibility, or natural variation. Compare a physical sample under the lighting used in your shop or
            product photography. For repeat orders, keep an approved reference sample and confirm whether any material
            or process has changed before production.
          </p>
          <a class="btn-primary mt-7 self-start" href="/contact">Request a sample discussion</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-[#0f3b2f] text-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-8 lg:flex-row lg:items-center lg:justify-between">
        <div class="max-w-2xl">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-[#d8c5a3]">
            Need exact material confirmation?
          </p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold">Send us the product reference and your material requirements.</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-white/80">
            Include the destination market, quantity, intended user, required metal grade or bead material, finish,
            packaging, and any testing requirement. We will help clarify what must be verified before quotation and
            sampling.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a
          class="inline-flex shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-white px-6 py-3.5 text-sm font-semibold text-[#0f3b2f]"
          href="/contact"
        >
          Discuss your sourcing brief
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
